\section{Data Collection}
\label{chap_3}

To systematically assess the correlation between evaluation metrics and human rankings for abstractive summarization, we engage human annotators to evaluate summaries generated by large language models (LLMs). Our data collection evaluates document summaries in eight languages, chosen to represent four typological families with both low- and high-resource languages within each group. The annotators rank the summaries along two quality dimensions: \textit{coherence}, which assesses the summaries’ grammaticality and readability, and \textit{completeness}, which measures the degree to which they capture the text's main ideas. 


