% Our findings highlight the sensitivity of the evaluation n-gram metrics to the language type at hand, favoring
% % \reut{n-gram metrics? or all metrics?}
% isolating and agglutinative over fusional languages. We demonstrate that tokenization considerations can significantly improve n-gram metrics correlation with human scores for fusional languages. Additionally, metrics like COMET, specifically trained for evaluation tasks, consistently outperform n-gram-based metrics and show better results for low-resource languages. We recommend moving away from n-gram metrics in fusional languages and call for more investment in neural-based metrics trained on human judgments for generation evaluation.

%Based on our analyses --- which 
Our analyses highlight the limited ability of n-gram-based metrics to handle complex linguistic structures—particularly those found in fusional languages—compared to neural network-based metrics, especially ones trained for multilingual evaluation of generative models. Based on these findings, we recommend transitioning from n-gram metrics to neural models specifically trained for multilingual summarization. As a possible mitigation during this transition, when using n-gram metrics for fusional languages, we suggest employing tokenization techniques that break down complex linguistic units.








