\begin{proposition}
    Every steering is planar.
    %and its plane embedding is unique up to combinatorial equivalence.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
    Let $H$ be a $(S, P)$-steering. A planar embedding of $H$ can be obtained in the
    following manner. First draw the cycle $H[S]$. Then choose one of the two faces of $H[S]$
    %\todo{Side or face?}
    and draw $H[P]$ arbitrarily in the interior of the chosen face. 
    Let $\pi$ be the permutation on $S$ representing the clockwise order
    of $S$ around this chosen face.
    If $P$ is a singleton $\{u\}$, we can straightforwardly draw edges between
    $u$ and members of $S$ without crossings in accordance with the
    order prescribed by $\pi$. 

    Suppose $|P| \geq 2$
    and let $u_1$ and $u_2$ be the ends of the path $H[P]$
    and let $R_i = N_H(u_i) \cap S$ for $i \in \{1, 2\}$. 
    Due to Condition 2 in the definition of steerings, $\{R_1, R_2\}$ is a socket of $H[S]$.
    Due to Condition 3, $N_H(P \setminus \{u_1, u_2\}) \cap S$ is either empty
    or $\{s\}$ where $s$ is a hinge of the socket $\{R_1, R_2\}$.
    Suppose the latter. Since $s$ is a hinge of the socket $\{R_1, R_2\}$,  
    $R_1$ is either $\{s_1\}$ or $\{s_1, s\}$ and $R_2$ is either $\{s_2\}$ or $\{s_2, s\}$,
    where $s_1$ and $s_2$ are two neighbors of $s$ on the cycle $H[S]$.
    We assume that $s_1$, $s$, and $s_2$ appear on $H[S]$ in this clockwise order around
    the region containing $P$.
    We order the edges between $P$ and $S$ in accordance with the
    clockwise order of $S$ prescribed by $\pi$: first the edges between $u_1$ and $S$,
    then the edges between $P \setminus \{u_1, u_2\}$ and $s$, and 
    finally the edges between $u_2$ and $S$. Following this order, 
    we can draw edges between $P$ and $S$ without crossings.
    The drawing for the case $N_H(P \setminus \{u_1, u_2\}) \cap S = \emptyset$ 
    is similar and simpler.
    We conclude that $H$ is planar.
    % 
 %   In embeddings for a fixed $\pi$, the clockwise order of $N_H(v)$
 %   around $v$ is unique for every $v \in V(H)$. Since, we have only two choices
 %   of the clockwise order $\pi$ of $S$, one being the inverse of the other,
 %   all planar embeddings of $H$ are combinatorially equivalent to each other.
\end{proof}

The following lemma shows that $X$ is a PMC of a triconnected plane graph $G$ if $L_G[X]$ is a steering. Before going into technical details, it may be helpful to study the examples in Figures~\ref{fig:steerings} and \ref{fig:steerings_bipartitions} and confirm the following.
\begin{enumerate}
\item The cycle bounding each face is chordless.
\item Every pair of vertices is either adjacent to each other or incident to a common face.
\end{enumerate}
The first non-steering graph in Figure~\ref{fig:non-steering}
does not satisfy Condition 1, having a face bounded by a cycle of length six going through the center vertex. This graph cannot be
$L_G[X]$ for a PMC $X$ since the component of $G[V(G) \setminus X]$
that lies in the face in question would not be separated by a minimal separator. The second non-steering graph in the same figure has
two vertices, the white vertex on the left and the right bottom black vertex, that are neither adjacent to each other nor share a common face. If $L_G[X]$ is this graph, then the third condition 
for $X$ to be a PMC in Lemma~\ref{lem:BT-lemma} would be violated. The third example has a similar problem.
\begin{lemma}
\label{lem:steering-is-PMC}
    Let $X$ be a vertex set of a triconnected plane graph $G$ and
    suppose $L_G[X]$ is a steering. Then, $X$ is a PMC of $G$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Let $H = L_G[X]$ and let $(S, P)$ be a bipartition of $X$ such that 
$H$ is an $(S, P)$-steering.
Since $L_G[S] = H[S]$ is a cycle, which we denote by $\gamma_0$,
$L_G[S]$ has exactly two regions
$r_0$ and $r$, one of which, say $r$ contains $P$.
Since $r_0$ does not contain
any vertex of $X$, $r_0$ is also a region of $L_G[X]$.
Let $R = N_{L_G}(P) \cap S$ and 
let $E$ be the set of edges of $L_G[X]$ between 
    $P$ and $S$. Due to the definition of a steering,
    $R$ is not a slot of $L_G[S]$ and hence $2 \leq |R| \leq |E|$. 
    The edges in $E$ divide the
    region $r$ of $L_G[S]$ into $k = |E|$ regions of $L_G[X]$ each bounded by a cycle
    consisting of two edges from $E$ 
    together possibly with some edges of $L_G[S]$ and possibly with
    some edges of $L_G[P]$. Let $\Gamma$ denote the set of these $k$ cycles.

    We show that each $\gamma \in \Gamma \cup \{\gamma_0\}$ is a chordless cycle of $L_G$. Since $S$ is a minimal separator of $G$, $\gamma_0 = L_G[S]$ is a chordless cycle of $L_G$, due to Proposition~\ref{prop:min-sep-of-triconn}. Let $\gamma$ be an arbitrary member of $\Gamma$. If $\gamma$ has a chord $\{u, v\}$ in $L_G$,
    then $\{u, v\}$ is an edge of $L_G[X]$ since $u, v \in X$. We show that no edge of $L_G[X]$ can be
    a chord of $\gamma$. We have three cases to consider: (1) $\{u, v\}$ is
    an edge of $\gamma_0$; (2) $\{u, v\} \in E$; (3) $\{u, v\}$ is an edge of the path $L_G[P]$.
    For (1), observe that the vertices in $V(\gamma) \cap S$ appear consecutively on $\gamma_0$.
    Therefore, for $\{u, v\}$ to be a chord of $\gamma$, $\gamma$ has to contain the subpath
    of $\gamma_0$ between $u$ and $v$ avoiding the edge $\{u, v\}$. However, this is impossible
    since $R$, being not a slot of $\gamma_0$, contains a vertex $w \not\in \{u, v\}$ 
    and $E$ contains an edge incident to $w$. 
    In cases (2) and (3), at most one of $u$ and $v$ belongs to $\gamma_0$ and therefore $\{u, v\}$
    is not a chord of $\gamma_0$. For each $\gamma \in \Gamma$, if $u, v \in V(\gamma)$
    then edge $\{u, v\}$ belongs to $\gamma$ and therefore is not a chord of $\gamma$. 
    We conclude that every 
    $\gamma \in \Gamma \cup \{\gamma_0\}$ is a chordless cycle of $L_G$. 

    We show that $X$ satisfies the three conditions for PMCs:
    (1) There is no full component associated with $X$;
    (2) Every member of $\CC_G(X)$ is minimally separated;
    (3) For every pair of vertices $x$ and $y$ in $X$,
    either $x$ is adjacent to $y$ in $G$ or there is some $C \in \CC_G(X)$
    such that $x, y \in N_G(C)$.

    Condition (1) holds, since each member of $\CC_G(X)$ is contained in one
    of the regions bounded by 
    cycles $\gamma \in \Gamma \cup \{\gamma_0\}$ 
    (Proposition~\ref{prop:compo_in_region})
    and $V(\gamma)$ is a proper subset of $X$
    for each such $\gamma$.
    For Condition (2), let $C$ be an arbitrary member of $\CC_G(X)$.
    Then, the region of $L_G[X]$
    that contains $C$ is bounded by a chordless cycle 
    $\gamma \in \Gamma \cup \{\gamma_0\}$ of $L_G$.
    Since one side
    of this cycle contains $C$ and the other side is also non-empty
    because it contains some vertices of $X$, $V(\gamma)$ is a minimal separator of $G$ due to Proposition~\ref{prop:min-sep-of-triconn}.
    Therefore, $C$ is minimally separated.

    To show that Condition (3) holds, 
    we first show that, for arbitrary two members $x$ and $y$ of $X$,
    there is some $\gamma \in \Gamma \cup \{\gamma_0\}$ such that
    $x, y \in V(\gamma)$. If $x, y \in V(\gamma_0)$ then this is trivial so
    let $x \in X \setminus V(\gamma_0)$ and let $y \in X$ be arbitrary.
    First suppose that $P$ is a singleton $\{u\}$, $x = u$, and
    $y \in V(\gamma_0)$. Then, we have $x, y \in V(\gamma)$ for some $\gamma \in \Gamma$, 
    since every $v \in S$ belongs to $V(\gamma)$ for some $\gamma \in \Gamma$
    and $u$ belongs to $V(\gamma)$ for every $\gamma \in \Gamma$. 
    Next suppose that $|P| \geq 2$. Let $u_1$ and $u_2$ be two ends of $L_G[P]$ and
    let $R_i = N_{L_G}(u_i) \cap S$ for $i \in \{1, 2\}$.
    Due to the definition of a steering, $\{R_1, R_2\}$ is a socket of $\gamma_0$.
    Let $I = P \setminus \{u_1, u_2\}$ be the set of internal vertices of the path $L_G[P]$.
    Due to the definition of a steering, $N_{L_G}(I) \cap S$ is either empty or
    a singleton $\{s\}$ where $s$ is a hinge of the socket $\{R_1, R_2\}$.
    Suppose first that $N_{L_G}(I) \cap S = \emptyset$.
    Then, the two slots $R_1$ and $R_2$ divide the cycle $\gamma_0$ into two 
    subpaths $p_1$ and $p_2$ each between a vertex in $R_1$ and a vertex in $R_2$
    with all internal vertices belonging to $V(\gamma_0) \setminus (R_1 \cup R_2)$. Since $R_1$ and $R_2$ are slots of $\gamma_0$, 
    we have $V(p_1) \cup V(p_2) = V(\gamma_0)$.
    We have a cycle $\gamma_i \in \Gamma$ formed by $p_i$ and $L_G[P]$ together with
    two edges from $E$, for $i \in \{1, 2\}$. 
    Since $S \subseteq V(\gamma_1) \cup V(\gamma_2)$ and 
    $P \subseteq V(\gamma_1) \cap V(\gamma_2)$, for every pair 
    $(x, y)$ of members of $X$, either $x, y \in V(\gamma_0)$, $x, y \in V(\gamma_1)$,
    or $x, y \in V(\gamma_2)$.

    We turn to the case where $N_{L_G}(I) \cap S = \{s\}$, where $s$ is a hinge of the socket
    $\{R_1, R_2\}$.
    Let $p$ be the subpath of the cycle $\gamma_0$ induced by $V(\gamma_0) \setminus \{s\}$.
    Since $R_1 \cup R_2$ is not a slot, 
    one end of $p$ is in $R_1$, the other end is in $R_2$, and no internal vertex of
    $p$ is in $R_1$ or $R_2$.
    This path $p$ and the path $L_G[P]$, together with two edges in $E$, 
    form a cycle $\gamma_1 \in \Gamma$. 
    %Note that every cycle in $\Gamma \setminus \{\gamma_0\}$ contains $s$.
    If we do not have both $x$ and $y$ in
    $\gamma_1$, then one of $x$ and $y$, say $x$, equals $s$. Since we are assuming
    that not both of $x$ and $y$ are in $V(\gamma_0)$, we have $y \in P$.
    Since every $\gamma \in \Gamma \setminus \{\gamma_1\}$ contains $s$ and
    every vertex in $P$ is contained in some $\gamma \in \Gamma \setminus \{\gamma_1\}$,
    it follows that $x, y \in \gamma$ for some $\gamma \in \Gamma \setminus \{\gamma_1\}$.
    We conclude that, for every pair of two members $x$ and $y$ of $X$, 
    there is some $\gamma \in \Gamma \cup \{\gamma_0\}$ such that $x, y \in V(\gamma)$.

    We are ready to show that Condition (3) holds. 
    Let $x$ and $y$ be arbitrary two vertices 
    in $X$. As we have shown above, there is a cycle $\gamma$ bounding a region
    of $L_G[X]$ such that $x, y \in V(\gamma)$. If this region contains some
    $C \in \CC_G(X)$, then we are done since $N_G(C) = V(\gamma)$.
    Otherwise, $\gamma$ is a triangle bounding an empty region and
    hence $\{x, y\}$ is an edge of $L_G[X]$. If this is an edge of $G$ then
    we are done, so suppose not. Let $\gamma'$ be another
    cycle bounding a region of $L_G[X]$ that contains the edge $\{x, y\}$.
    This region bounded by $\gamma'$ cannot be empty since, if it were,
    then we would have a face of $G$ incident
    to four or more vertices of $X$, a contradiction to the assumption that 
    $L_G[X]$ is a steering and hence is a plane graph.
    We are done, since $x, y \in N_G(C')$ where $C'$ is the member of $\CC_G(X)$ contained
    in the region bounded by $\gamma'$.
\end{proof}

