The scheduling language in SWIRL supports three types of optimizations: (1) data layout transformations, such as transpose or privatize; (2) loop transformations (e.g., tile and unroll-and-jam), along with vectorization and multi-threading applied within a layer; and, (3) loop fusion across layers.
SWIRL achieves performance on par with highly optimized vendor libraries from Intel, but supports only CPU hardware.  Autotuning is used to explore tile sizes and unroll factors as well as evaluate different code variants.
