\subsection{Excursion: Integrated Autotuning}
\label{sec_excursion_at}


Scheduling languages facilitate a systematic exploration across alternative specifications to locate
an optimized schedule, generating code variants from a compact specification of optimizations.  As the best optiimization strategy is dependent on both the architecture and the input data set, 
%Still, for a specific program, 
the optimized sequence of transformations described by the scheduling language is often hard to find.   
This section discusses different ways in which frameworks that use scheduling languages are combined with techniques to explore a search space of potential optimization strategies.
We focus on two aspects.  First, we consider how the possible optimization sequences are determined and specified, which we refer to as \textit{defining the optimization search space}.  A \textit{point in the search space} is then an instantiated schedule that can be provided to the scheduling language to generate code.  
These search spaces are typically prohibitively large, and it is impractical to evaluate each point in the search space.  Therefore, various techniques to limit the search space and sample a subset of points is needed, which we refer to as \textit{search space pruning}.  

A very common approach to defining the optimization search space 
%The first step is to generate a sequence of schedules. One approach 
is to use manually-written scheduling templates, written by performance experts, with variables embedded in each template that are placeholders for parameters in the search space; e.g., in frameworks for deep learning such as AutoTVM~\cite{chen2018learning} and SWIRL~\cite{SWIRL}. In domain-specific systems, where the set of possible optimizations is well understood, this approach can be effective because the search space can be kept narrow. 

In other systems, the search space is generated by an algorithmic procedure, and a point in the search space is evaluated using a variety of techniques.  A discussion of the search algorithms is beyond the scope of this paper, but we focus here on different cost models for evaluating a point in the search space.  \textit{Performance models} can be used to compute a metric associated with each point, with the algorithm selecting a code variant associated with the best value of the metric.   Alternatively, \textit{autotuning}
is frequently used to measure the execution time of an optimized code variant running on a specific hardware platform.  A third option is to build a \textit{predictive model} that derives a schedule using deep learning: a learning phase typically derives an associated cost with different schedules and inputs, and inference performs a lookup into the model at runtime.  


