The objective function for this example is defined as
\begin{equation}
F(x_1, x_2) = 
\begin{cases} 
(x_1 + 5)^2 + |x_2| & \text{if } x_1 < -5 \, , \\
-2 \, \sin(x_1) + |x_2| & \text{if } -5 \leq x_1 < -3 \, , \\
0.5 \, x_1 + 2 + |x_2| & \text{if } -3 \leq x_1 < 0 \, , \\
0.3 \, \sqrt{x_1} + 2.5 + |x_2| & \text{if } x_1 \geq 0 \, .
\end{cases}
\end{equation}

The constraints include one inequality and one equality conic constraint given by
\begin{align}
G(x_1, x_2) & = 2x_1^2 + x_2^2 - 3 \leq 0 \, , \\
H(x_1, x_2) &= (x_1 + 1)^2 - \left(\frac{x_2}{2}\right)^4 = 0 \, .
\end{align}

These constraints define a feasible region that is both non-convex and challenging due to the presence of an equality constraint that introduces a higher-order nonlinearity.

The MATLAB code, provided in \texttt{MainCEoptExample6.m} and available on GitHub, implements the \pkg{CEopt} solver to minimize the objective function while satisfying the constraints.


