\section{A Randomized Controlled Experiment}\label{randomized_experiment}
We designed a randomized controlled experiment for a junior developer vacancy that compares two recruitment pipelines. In the conventional resume-based pipeline, a standard resume-scoring algorithm assigns each applicant a \textit{Resume Score}, which human recruiters then review before deciding whom to invite to a live interview. The \emph{Resume Score} is a conventional measure of a candidate's suitability for the role, and it is frequently used by recruiters in selection decisions. In the AI-enabled pipeline, applicants complete a fully conversational video interview administered by \textit{AI Recruiter}. The system generates a structured report for each candidate, and recruiters use this information, rather than a resume score, to decide who advances to the live interview. All candidates who reach the interview stage are assessed by the same human interviewers (blind to the earlier selection process), and passing that interview is our primary outcome of interest. Additionally, we track the highest-ranked candidates from both pipelines for five months after the experiment, recording whether they secure positions outside the micro1 posting.

\subsection{Experiment Design and Implementation} micro1 advertised a junior front-end engineer vacancy on LinkedIn. The position required proficiency in React, JavaScript, and CSS. Over 36 thousand candidates applied. Candidates were randomly assigned to treatment (AI process) and control (conventional process) groups using a 70/30 split without stratification. Figure \ref{experimental_pipeline} illustrates the experimental design and workflow. Unequal assignment probabilities were implemented to account for anticipated attrition within the treatment group. 

All treatment candidates received an invitation to participate in the AI interview; 5,560 completed the interview, and 286 met micro1's formal pass thresholds. Passing required the following criteria: at least \emph{Mid-level}  in all three assessed skills, an LLM-generated proctoring score, indicating the likelihood of not cheating, of 70\% or higher, received an AI-soft skills rating of at least \emph{Mid-level}, and  at least two years of relevant experience. From these 286 candidates, recruiters manually disqualified those they judged as cheating (if they were seen to be using other tabs/devices during the interview or did not have their video on). Candidates in the treatment group also received \emph{Resume Scores} independently of the AI interview, but these scores were not used in the selection process.

In the control group, all applications were reviewed using an AI resume scoring algorithm.\footnote{A standard keyword based ML algorithm offered by micro1 was used to assign \textit{Resume Score}. We additionally checked robustness by applying a popular open source resume scoring method \textit{Resume Matcher} -- https://github.com/srbhr/Resume-Matcher.} \textit{Resume Score} takes values from 0 to 100; 1,400 candidates received the maximum \textit{Resume Score}. Recruiters observed the \textit{Resume Score}s and the rest of the application materials and selected candidates for the human interview round.

35 candidates from each pipeline were scheduled for the final interview. Next, a human interviewer received the application materials of the selected candidates and conducted the final interview. The interviewer was blind to the pipeline through which each candidate had advanced. The final outcomes of the human interviews were whether candidates passed and received an offer or were rejected. 

Finally, five months later, between June 1st and 8th, we collected LinkedIn profiles of 3,457 candidates, including all candidates that passed the AI interview and a randomly selected group from the remaining applicants. We used job titles and employment starting dates to create an outcome variable \emph{new job}, which takes the value of 1 if the candidate reported a job with a starting date after our experiment began. We excluded jobs with 'freelance' in the job title.



