\subsection{Average Treatment Effects.} 
The average treatment effect (ATE) is defined as:

\begin{align}
    \tau = \mathbb{E}\left[Y | W = 1, Z = 1 \right] - \mathbb{E}\left[Y | W = 0, Z = 1 \right],
\end{align}
where  $Y$ refers to the outcome of the human interview (pass or fail) or acquisition of a \emph{New Job}, $W$ to treatment assignment, and $Z$ to the first stage outcome. When considering the primary outcome, $Z = 1$ means that the candidate was selected for the final stage human interview. In LinkedIn data analysis, $Z = 1$ indicates passing the AI interview in the treatment group or having the top \emph{Resume Score} in the control group.

\paragraph{Human Interview Stage.} We are interested in comparing the average rate of passing the human interview across candidates selected from the treatment and control groups. 

To estimate the average treatment effect, we consider three estimators: difference-in-means, Ordinary Least Squares (OLS), where we adjust for all observed characteristics, and Augmented Inverse Propensity Matching (AIPW) in which we additionally adjust for treatment propensity. We use the Generalized Random Forest implementation of the AIPW \citep{wager2018estimation}. Table \ref{tab:ate_method} presents the results.

