We estimate an average treatment effect of 20 pp. (SE 12 pp.); when adjusting for the observed covariates, we estimate the ATE of 26 pp. (SE 12 pp.). Thus, the treatment group is 60\% more likely to pass the human interview than the control group.\footnote{The covariate adjustments changes the estimate substantially because the candidates from the two groups systematically differ in their observed characteristics; these characteristics impact both the decisions to be included in the human interview stage and are correlated with the outcome of this stage.} 

\paragraph{New Jobs Reported on LinkedIn.} We next analyze the outcome data collected from LinkedIn.  Applying to the micro1 position suggests that the candidate was searching for a new job; thus, reporting a new employment might indicate a positive outcome. All jobs with a starting date before January, 2025 (date of the application to micro1) count as not a new job; thus, before January, the outcome is 0 for everyone. We consider two subsets of candidates. First,  we compare the rates of new job reports for the 70 candidates selected for the second-stage human interview. Second, we expand the set of considered candidates to include all candidates that passed the AI interview and candidates in the control group who received the top \emph{Resume Score}.\footnote{\emph{Resume Score} takes value from 0 to 100. The top score in our sample is 98.75. 1400 candidates were assigned this score.} Figure \ref{fig:linkedin_jobs} visualizes the results. The left panel focuses on the subset that participated in the human interviews; the right panel is the expanded group. The patterns of outcomes indicate that the treatment group is increasingly more likely to report having a new job on their LinkedIn profile relative to the control group.


