\subsection{Resume vs. AI-Vetted Skill Verification}\label{mechanism_3}

Now we investigate whether \emph{AI Recruiter} identifies instances of candidates claiming skills they do not possess.  Prior research documents widespread exaggeration of technical competencies: \citet{henle2019assessing} estimate that more than half of applications contain deliberate skill inflation, and the Dunning–Kruger bias implies that candidates often over-rate their own proficiency \citep{kruger1999unskilled}.  These distortions raise verification costs and hiring risks for firms.

Software‐engineering resumes routinely enumerate a large catalogue of technologies, frameworks, and tools.  For illustration, a single profile
might list:  

\begin{adjustwidth}{1.5em}{1.5em}  
\noindent\texttt{Skills: JavaScript, React, Angular, Next.js, Node.js,
TypeScript, GraphQL, Git, Cypress, Playwright, Selenium, MongoDB, MySQL,
PL/SQL, Docker, React Native, Expo, Express, Firebase, Puppeteer, Appium,
Gatsby.js, Styled Components, CSS Modules, Knex, Apollo, React Hook Form,
Redux, Redux-Saga, Formik, Reselect, TypeORM, AirTable, PHP, Ruby,
Cucumber, Ag-Grid, Plaid API, SCRUM, Google Data Studio, Jira, SOAPUI,
Postman, Redux Thunk}.
\end{adjustwidth}

\noindent 

In our experimental sample, resumes contain 23 skills on average, underscoring both the breadth of self‐reported expertise and the challenge recruiters face when attempting to verify each claim. Structured, competency-based interviews have long been recommended to narrow the gap between stated and actual skills \citep{schmidt1998validity}.  We evaluate whether \emph{AI Recruiter} fulfills this role by comparing the skills listed on resumes with those inferred by the model.  A large discrepancy would indicate that the interview’s predictive success stems, in part, from detecting inflated self-reports.

Table \ref{tab:skill_deficiency} presents two complementary measures. Columns 1–2 use the subset of the treatment group of the randomized experiment that participated in the AI interview and flag candidates as \emph{misreporting} if the AI interview rates \emph{any} of the three required skills: React, JavaScript, or CSS as \emph{Not familiar}.\footnote{Recall that the \emph{AI Recruiter} tests for three skills selected by the employer. In our case, the three skills tested by the AI interview and listed as required in the job listing were React, JavaScript, and CSS.}  Roughly 21.3 percent of treated candidates who participated in the AI interview fail this minimal screen (SE\ 1.5 \%). Furthermore, 12.4\% of candidates misreported two skills and 7.5\% all three skills.

To gain additional robustness and test whether the phenomenon of skill misreporting is not unique to the three skills tested in the randomized experiment, we take a random sample from micro1 historical data and repeat the analysis. Columns 3–4, match AI skill ratings to resume strings for 720 historical candidates, and record a \emph{misreport} whenever a required technology appears on the resume but the AI classifies the candidate as \textit{Not familiar}. The audit reveals misrepresentation in 21.6 percent of cases (SE\ 0.5 \%); 10.4 percent misreport at least two skills, and 5.2 percent claim proficiency in all three technologies despite the AI detecting none of them.


