As a first baseline, we select a sentence tokenizer, treating each sentence in the source paragraph as a claim. Next, we select two baselines \cite{pan-etal-2021-zero, wright-etal-2022-generating} that use a pipeline with two transformer models to generate claims from the source paragraph through entity extraction. The first model generates a question-answer pair, where the answer is the entity, while the second reformulates it as a claim. We also introduce a method where we propose to build the claims around noun phrases rather than named entities.

To assess the performance of the claim generation methods we compute a similarity score using a DeBERTa model fine-tuned on the STS-B dataset\footnote{https://huggingface.co/bobox/DeBERTa2-0.9B-ST-stsb}. We compare the most similar generated claim with each ground truth claim extracted from the same source paragraph. If there is not any ground truth claim that surpasses our Levenshtein threshold for a given generated claim, the claim is not taken into account. Table \ref{table:cg_sim_scores} shows that SciClaims %, which leverages a large language model (LLM), 
achieves the highest score on SciFact and RoSE, outperforming the baselines. However, SciClaims is slightly outperformed by \cite{pan-etal-2021-zero} in FEVER, suggesting that our prompt may need additional refinement to generalize to broader domains. Appendix \ref{sec:metrics} reports more extensive results. 

\subsubsection{Claim verification}

The verification task involves predicting the veracity of claim-evidence pairs, classifying each as \textit{SUPPORT}, \textit{REFUTE}, or \textit{NOT ENOUGH INFORMATION (NEI)}. Thus, this module is evaluated using the FEVER \cite{thorne-etal-2018-fever} and SciFact \cite{wadden-etal-2020-fact} datasets, where annotated claims-evidence pairs serve as ground truth. Additionally, we use CLIMATE-FEVER \cite{diggelmann2021climatefeverdatasetverificationrealworld}, a domain-specific dataset focusing on climate science. 

