\subsubsection{Positiveness}
    It’s brief to prove that the $\mathcal{G}(\boldsymbol{Y})=y^\star_j$ is a positive function.
\subsubsection{Monotonicity}
It’s easy to understand this function $\frac{\partial W}{\partial \boldsymbol{Y}} >0$. Hence, we use the chain rule in the derivatives in two parts: $\frac{\partial \mathcal{G}(\boldsymbol{Y})}{\partial \boldsymbol{Y}} =\frac{\partial \mathcal{G}(\boldsymbol{Y})}{\partial W} \frac{\partial W}{\partial \boldsymbol{Y}}$. The first derivative of $\mathcal{G}(\boldsymbol{Y})$ in terms of $W$ is
